#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128938 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8cca7ba8-24dc-5968-99a4-8d08592954fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6225" :question/problem "What are the spherical polar coordinates of the point $P$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1982/Source/Images/f1f3dd486bff704208c9340c8937defb.png)" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$, where\n\n$$\n\\rho \\geq 0\n$$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi\n$$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $P$ equals $4$ units.\n- The half-plane that contains the point $P$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $P$ forms an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(4, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{10}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128939 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbfe5ff1-14d6-5e0f-87be-3c9552f7868f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Spherical Coordinates of a Point Into the Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "7007e89d-fe92-5cc9-a0d1-49f34036176f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6226" :question/problem "The point $Q$ has spherical coordinates $\\left(2\\sqrt3, \\pi, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$." :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos (π)\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\sin (π)\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= ρ\\cos ϕ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-\\sqrt3, 0, -3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "807f1cec-5e90-50be-afd1-0bf627c50fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Converting the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Into the Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "cadaa861-db49-58bc-97a5-42c3dda4fa75" :question/math-academy-id "e-6227" :question/problem "The point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt2,0,\\sqrt2)$. What are the spherical polar coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "If $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho$, $\\theta$, and $\\phi$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad \\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nρ &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2} + 0^{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2 + 0 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (-\\sqrt2,0)\n$$\n\nlies on the negative $x$-axis, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\phi$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ &= \\arccos (\\frac{z}{ρ}) \\\\\n&= \\arccos (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2, \\pi, \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28a9233c-6ff6-5ed0-b59b-e7f9807b4ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Coordinates of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90ce1e80-3187-5483-b3d9-f92b8a8a18af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{π}{3},-\\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128595} {:answer/id #uuid "968dde5b-b165-5a3c-add4-b373ea2158b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{3},-\\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128596} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7e67c-f93d-51fc-a7f4-9ba66bc16b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128597} {:answer/id #uuid "19008d38-8a34-501d-a63d-9b1773792dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{π}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128598} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2789ea-9587-5da3-bd77-8052bfcbc6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc7e67c-f93d-51fc-a7f4-9ba66bc16b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, \\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{π}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128597} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ba7f1f8-ab48-52dc-97d4-e15b17d6f6d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128594}] :question/id #uuid "6ff8d6ca-d0bb-5b1c-8a89-3e8d60a00c7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-